            <ArticleTitle>Asthma in the elderly--a more severe disease.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>579-81</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To determine the severity of asthma in the elderly compared to the younger asthmatics.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Cross-sectional study.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">University outpatient asthma clinic.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SUBJECTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Asthmatics seen over a 6 month period in 1997.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">There were 154 patients and 16% were elderly (&gt; 65 years) asthmatics. The elderly asthmatics were on significantly more anti-asthmatic medications (2.3 +/- 1.1 vs. 1.6 +/- 0.9, p &lt; 0.001), and their clinical severity was significantly worse than their younger counterparts (Step 2.2 +/- 1.2 vs. 1.7 +/- 1.0, p &lt; 0.001). Near-fatal asthma episodes were also more common in the elderly asthmatics (39% vs 13%, chi 2 test p &lt; 0.01).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Elderly asthmatics appear to have more severe asthma as evidenced by the increase in near-fatal episodes, and their increased clinical severity.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
